the ceasefire between houthi rebels and  yemen's internationally recognised  government was first agreed in april  it'll now last for another two months  yemenis have greeted the news with  relief and hope  yemenis are mostly optimistic about the  results we hope the truce will be  extended and other issues that haven't  been resolved over the past period will  be addressed as well including the  opening of roads and crossings  the war in yemen started in 2014 when  iran backed houthi rebels took control  of the capital sanaa forcing the  internationally recognised government to  flee  the saudi coalition joined the war the  following year to try to restore the  government to power more than 150 000  people have been killed during eight  years of fighting u.s president joe  biden and the un have welcomed the move  to extend the truce  the longer we can create  a uh  and keep an a kind of truth bubble  the more we hope  that donors  will continue to fund our operations the  conflicts created one of the world's  worst humanitarian crises the un says  more than 4 million people have been  displaced 19 million others are going  hungry analysts say the truce is a  pathway to peace the institution of a  new presidential council in yemen i  think that's been key because this new  council uh an eight-man council has  really been focused on trying to change  the trajectory of yemen and it was  significant that some of the old war  mongers were removed from leadership  positions in order for this council to  come in so there's everything to play  for now  there is still the problem with tyres a  large city in yemen's south west that's  still under siege  but after eight long years of fighting  and misery there is at least hope among  yemenis that this devastating war might  finally be coming to an end  victoria gatenbee al jazeera
 

